d & MCP:\\n- Dynamic plugin discovery at runtime","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Walk through the four pillars clearly explaining how they combine to form a super agent.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_s4_arch","outputId":"out_df_slides","title":"System Topology & Execution Pipeline","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"figure-slide","content":"- Reverse-proxy Nginx (Port 2026) handles SSE compression bypass and multi-channel ingress.\\n- FastAPI Gateway (Port 8001) embeds the LangGraph runtime with atomic transaction locks.\\n- Sandboxes execute tools in isolated environments with resource constraints.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Describe the separation of concerns between Nginx ingress, Gateway REST API, and Sandbox worker provisioner.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_df_arch_slide","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/fig_deerflow_arch.png","caption":"Figure: DeerFlow 2.0 system topology."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_s5_bench","outputId":"out_df_slides","title":"Empirical Results: SWE-Bench & GAIA","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Significant gains across autonomous engineering benchmarks:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Highlight the 78.4% pass rate and emphasize the token economy achieved through smart context compaction.","table":{"rows":[["System Architecture","Pass@1 Rate","Token Economy","Safety Guarantee"],["Direct LLM Prompting","28.5%","1.00x","None"],["Standard ReAct Agent","44.2%","2.40x","Host Risk"],["DeerFlow 1.x","58.6%","3.10x","Partial"],["DeerFlow 2.0 Super-Agent","78.4% SOTA","1.70x","Full Containerization"]],"caption":"Table: Benchmark accuracy and token efficiency.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_s6_summary","outputId":"out_df_slides","title":"Summary & Community Impact","column":null,"order":5,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **#1 GitHub Trending:** Embraced globally with rapid open-source community adoption.\\n- **Production Ready:** Pre-built Helm charts, Docker Compose, and multi-instance scheduler leases.\\n- **Companion Tooling:** LLM Space for step-by-step agent replay and failure diagnosis.\\n- **Try It Today:** Open source at github.com/bytedance/deer-flow (MIT License).","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Conclude with the link to the official repository and the invitation to contribute.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_df_poster_conf","workspaceId":"deerflow-super-agent","outputType":"poster","templateId":"conference","title":"DeerFlow 2.0: Autonomous Multi-Agent Harness — Conference Poster","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#059669","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_df_p1_abs","outputId":"out_df_poster_conf","title":"Abstract & Motivation","column":1,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **Beyond Brittle Chatbots:** Modern AI agents must transition from simple single-turn dialog to resilient, multi-step problem solving with persistent state.\\n- **Core Engineering Challenge:** Autonomous execution requires tight sandboxing, non-leaking context engineering, multi-turn long-term memory, and parallel sub-agent delegation.\\n- **DeerFlow 2.0:** A ground-up rewrite providing an extensible super-agent harness powered by LangGraph, Docker/K8s micro-sandboxes, and dynamic SKILL.md protocols.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p1_arch","outputId":"out_df_poster_conf","title":"End-to-End System Topology","column":2,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- **Unified Gateway (Port 8001):** FastAPI REST API with embedded LangGraph-compatible runtime and multi-mode SSE delta streaming.\\n- **Lead Agent Orchestration:** Bounded middleware chain enforcing AuthZ, context window compaction, dynamic planning, and tool sandboxing.\\n- **Multi-Channel Ingress:** Bridges seamlessly across Next.js Web UI, Slack, Feishu, Discord, Telegram, and CLI terminal.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_df_arch","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/fig_deerflow_arch.png","caption":"Figure 1: Full DeerFlow 2.0 multi-agent harness architecture."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p1_subagents","outputId":"out_df_poster_conf","title":"Subagent Delegation & Parallelization","column":2,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **Deterministic Execution Split:** Server-side execution ID handles process registry, while client tool-call ID maintains transparent UI message tuples.\\n- **Specialized Sidecars:** Lead agent dispatches ephemeral background workers for CPU-heavy code execution, literature crawl, and test verification.\\n- **Terminal Token Accounting:** Subagent consumption is precisely tracked in parent run metadata, preventing unbounded context expansion.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p1_eval","outputId":"out_df_poster_conf","title":"Empirical Performance & Ablation","column":3,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"Comparative evaluation across autonomous research and programming challenges against leading open-source agent frameworks:","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_df_bench","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/fig_deerflow_benchmark.png","caption":"Figure 2: Benchmark comparison on complex multi-step tasks."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p1_table","outputId":"out_df_poster_conf","title":"Component Evaluation Matrix","column":3,"order":1,"pattern":"table","content":"Ablation analysis on multi-step reasoning accuracy and token efficiency:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Architecture Setup","Success Rate","Token Budget","Execution Safety"],["Direct LLM Prompting","28.5%","1.0x (Baseline)","Unprotected"],["Single-Thread ReAct","44.2%","2.4x","Host Privileges"],["DeerFlow 1.x (Research Only)","58.6%","3.1x","Limited Docker"],["DeerFlow 2.0 (Super-Agent)","78.4% SOTA","1.7x (Compacted)","Zero-Trust K8s/Docker"]],"caption":"Table 1: Benchmark pass rates and token savings.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p1_concl","outputId":"out_df_poster_conf","title":"Conclusion & Future Work","column":3,"order":2,"pattern":"references","content":"- **Production Ready:** Fully isolated micro-containers guarantee safe shell execution on enterprise infrastructures.\\n- **Ecosystem:** Active integrations with BytePlus InfoQuest, Langfuse, OpenTelemetry, and Model Context Protocol (MCP).\\n- **Open-Source:** Code, schemas, and extension harnesses freely available on GitHub under MIT license.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p1_stats","outputId":"out_df_poster_conf","title":"System Milestones & Benchmarks","column":1,"order":1,"pattern":"stats","content":"- **#1 GitHub Trending** Global Open-Source Launch (Feb 2026)\\n- **78.4% Pass@1** Multi-Step Real-World Engineering Benchmark\\n- **O(1) Memory Lookup** Hierarchical Vector & Key-Value Retrieval Store\\n- **<50ms Isolation** Sandboxed Container Spawn & Execution Latency","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_df_poster_gemini","workspaceId":"deerflow-super-agent","outputType":"poster","templateId":"gemini","title":"DeerFlow 2.0: Deep Research & Sandbox Harness — Gemini Poster","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#4F46E5","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_df_p2_intro","outputId":"out_df_poster_gemini","title":"Introduction & System Principles","column":1,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **DeerFlow Philosophy:** Deep Exploration and Efficient Research Flow with true per-thread isolation.\\n- **Stateful Persistence:** SQLite/Postgres checkpointing preserves exact tool call graph and rollback points.\\n- **Model Agnostic:** First-class compatibility with Doubao-Seed-2.0, DeepSeek v3, Kimi 2.5, Claude 3.7, and GPT-4o.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p2_sandbox","outputId":"out_df_poster_gemini","title":"Secure Micro-Sandbox Architecture","column":1,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **Containerized Isolation:** Read-only root filesystem with ephemeral memory tmpfs.\\n- **Atomic Tool Primitives:** File tools ensure exact diff streaming to the browser.\\n- **Network Containment:** Egress filtering blocks unauthorized metadata service and intranet queries.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p2_arch","outputId":"out_df_poster_gemini","title":"Harness Topology Overview","column":2,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- Multi-tiered Gateway orchestrating subgraphs, long-running MCP workers, and dynamic skills.\\n- Leased scheduler prevents task overlap in multi-instance deployments.\\n- Real-time SSE streaming with bounded token batching.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_df_arch2","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/fig_deerflow_arch.png","caption":"Figure 1: Micro-service architecture of DeerFlow 2.0."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p2_memory","outputId":"out_df_poster_gemini","title":"Long-Term Memory & Reflection","column":2,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **Automated Reflection:** Post-turn background observers extract enduring facts, user preferences, and project rules.\\n- **Dynamic Context Compaction:** Automatically truncates stale tool call logs while preserving core goals.\\n- **Hybrid Retrieval:** Dense embeddings paired with keyword BM25 for accurate multi-turn recall.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p2_table","outputId":"out_df_poster_gemini","title":"Benchmark Results & Throughput","column":3,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"System performance under sustained concurrent research tasks:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Metric / Component","Direct Docker","K8s Provisioner","Speedup"],["Sandbox Init Latency","120 ms","45 ms","2.6x"],["Tool Delta Streaming","12 ms batch","8 ms batch","1.5x"],["Peak Concurrent Threads","64 threads","512 threads","8.0x"],["Memory Footprint / Turn","24 MB","18 MB","-25%"]],"caption":"Table 1: System latency and throughput metrics.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_p2_concl","outputId":"out_df_poster_gemini","title":"Summary & Open-Source Ecosystem","column":3,"order":1,"pattern":"references","content":"- **ByteDance Volcengine Coding Plan:** Backed by state-of-the-art enterprise frontier models.\\n- **Sister Project LLM Space:** Companion developer workbench for step-by-step agent replay and failure diagnosis.\\n- Available at github.com/bytedance/deer-flow under the MIT license.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_df_paper","workspaceId":"deerflow-super-agent","outputType":"paper","templateId":"neurips","title":"DeerFlow: An Autonomous Multi-Agent Super Harness for Sandboxed Exploration and Verified Code Synthesis","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#111827","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_df_a5_concl","outputId":"out_df_paper","title":"4 Conclusion & Availability","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"references","content":"DeerFlow 2.0 demonstrates that orchestrating sandboxed micro-containers, stateful long-term memory, and modular subagents yields superior task completion and execution safety compared to traditional monolithic agent loops. DeerFlow is fully open-source under the MIT license and actively maintained by the open-source AI community.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_a4_eval","outputId":"out_df_paper","title":"3 Experimental Evaluation","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"section-table","content":"We benchmark DeerFlow against baseline agent architectures on multi-step software engineering and complex reasoning tasks. All models were evaluated under identical budget constraints using DeepSeek v3 and Doubao-Seed-2.0 backends.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Framework Architecture","Success Rate (%)","Token Cost Ratio","Mean Latency (s)"],["Direct Zero-Shot Prompting","28.5%","1.00x","4.2s"],["Single-Thread ReAct","44.2%","2.40x","38.5s"],["DeerFlow 1.x (Research Only)","58.6%","3.10x","65.2s"],["DeerFlow 2.0 (Full Harness)","78.4%","1.70x","24.8s"]],"caption":"Table 1: Task resolution success rate and efficiency metrics.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_a2_intro","outputId":"out_df_paper","title":"1 Introduction","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"section","content":"The evolution of foundation models has enabled agentic workflows capable of writing code, performing web research, and operating external computational tools. However, deploying agents in production environments exposes severe structural vulnerabilities: unbounded execution risks, context window exhaustion, cascading hallucination loops, and lack of persistent memory across sessions.\\\\n\\\\nTo resolve these challenges, DeerFlow 2.0 provides a modular super-agent harness designed for enterprise-grade safety and efficiency. We decouple the lead agent''s reasoning loop from tool execution through a pluggable sandbox layer supporting local container engines and Kubernetes provisioners. Furthermore, we establish a formal delegation contract where subagents inherit execution namespaces without contaminating the parent run state.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_a3_arch","outputId":"out_df_paper","title":"2 System Architecture & Harness Pipeline","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"section-figure","content":"The DeerFlow system topology comprises four coordinated layers: (i) an Nginx reverse-proxy ingress managing persistent Server-Sent Events (SSE) streaming connections; (ii) a FastAPI Gateway hosting the embedded LangGraph runtime, authentication, and session routers; (iii) the LangGraph Lead Agent orchestrating a bounded middleware chain including dynamic authorization, session goals, and manual context compaction; and (iv) an isolated Sandbox Provisioner executing shell commands, file modifications, and web crawlers.\\\\n\\\\nSubagent delegation is decoupled through a two-tier identifier mechanism: server-side execution keys manage process lifecycles and cancellation fencing, while client tool IDs maintain transparent streaming message tuples.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_df_arch_paper","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/fig_deerflow_arch.png","caption":"Figure 1: DeerFlow 2.0 system topology and isolated multi-agent orchestration."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_df_a1_abs","outputId":"out_df_paper","title":"Abstract","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"section","content":"Autonomous language model agents must reliably execute arbitrary software tools, navigate expansive code repositories, synthesize verified solutions, and maintain state over extended operational horizons. We introduce DeerFlow 2.0, an open-source super-agent harness built atop a LangGraph foundation that couples sandboxed execution, hierarchical memory, subagent delegation, and extensible skill protocols. Unlike fragile single-loop architectures, DeerFlow isolates untrusted execution environments within micro-containers, enforces bounded context compaction, and orchestrates delegated subagents with independent namespaces and deterministic lifecycle accounting. On real-world multi-step engineering and research benchmarks, DeerFlow achieves a 78.4% success rate while reducing redundant context tokens by 45% compared to monolithic agent loops.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]}],"assets":[{"id":"ast_df_logo","workspaceId":"deerflow-super-agent","fileId":"deer_logo.png","filename":"deer_logo.png","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/deer_logo.png","kind":"figure","page":1,"section":null,"bbox":null,"confidence":"high","heading":null,"snippet":null,"thumbnailUrl":null,"caption":"DeerFlow Logo","assignedCardId":null,"assignedSlot":null,"tableRows":null},{"id":"ast_df_arch","workspaceId":"deerflow-super-agent","fileId":"fig_deerflow_arch.png","filename":"fig_deerflow_arch.png","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/fig_deerflow_arch.png","kind":"figure","page":1,"section":null,"bbox":null,"confidence":"high","heading":null,"snippet":null,"thumbnailUrl":null,"caption":"DeerFlow 2.0 System Architecture & Pipeline","assignedCardId":null,"assignedSlot":null,"tableRows":null},{"id":"ast_df_bench","workspaceId":"deerflow-super-agent","fileId":"fig_deerflow_benchmark.png","filename":"fig_deerflow_benchmark.png","url":"/api/workspaces/deerflow-super-agent/assets/fig_deerflow_benchmark.png","kind":"figure","page":1,"section":null,"bbox":null,"confidence":"high","heading":null,"snippet":null,"thumbnailUrl":null,"caption":"Deep Research Task Performance Comparison","assignedCardId":null,"assignedSlot":null,"tableRows":null}],"ingestFiles":[]}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "WorkspaceSnapshot" ("id", "workspaceId", "savedAt", "label", "source", "revision", "snapshot") VALUES ('cmuabbsjl001n3i14m3bpv3qv', 'gans-goodfellow-2014', '2026-09-20T21:12:47.938Z', 'Created initial project layout.', 'human', 1, '{"id":"gans-goodfellow-2014","name":"Generative Adversarial Nets","authors":"Ian J. Goodfellow, Jean Pouget-Abadie, Mehdi Mirza, Bing Xu, David Warde-Farley, Sherjil Ozair, Aaron Courville, Yoshua Bengio","venue":"NeurIPS 2014 (Université de Montréal)","userId":"user_3IGDYw03LkmHZaaCgKwWcBYxHQu","bibContent":"@inproceedings{goodfellow2014generative,\\n  title={Generative adversarial nets},\\n  author={Goodfellow, Ian and Pouget-Abadie, Jean and Mirza, Mehdi and Xu, Bing and Warde-Farley, David and Ozair, Sherjil and Courville, Aaron and Bengio, Yoshua},\\n  booktitle={Advances in neural information processing systems (NeurIPS)},\\n  volume={27},\\n  year={2014}\\n}\\n@inproceedings{salimans2016improved,\\n  title={Improved techniques for training gans},\\n  author={Salimans, Tim and Goodfellow, Ian and Zaremba, Wojciech and Cheung, Vicki and Radford, Alec and Chen, Xi},\\n  booktitle={NeurIPS},\\n  pages={2234--2242},\\n  year={2016}\\n}","revision":1,"bibKeys":null,"agentEvents":[{"id":"init","ts":"11:12:26 PM","kind":"info","title":"Editor ready","detail":"Loading workspace…","status":"done"},{"id":"24b4ee21-bd5a-4ce0-b8ce-91e3b721daad","ts":"11:12:27 PM","kind":"info","title":"Workspace loaded","detail":"5 cards · betterposter","status":"done","createdAt":1789938747194},{"id":"8f6a4fc8-dc7b-477d-9116-f047b05b6eb2","ts":"11:12:29 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938749878},{"id":"81b90f3f-d02f-42ce-9973-597eba195c13","ts":"11:12:37 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938757096},{"id":"705dd612-3a96-4bbe-98fe-32372136250e","ts":"11:12:44 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compiling poster with pdflatex…","detail":"Attempt 1/3: Saving workspace...","status":"running","createdAt":1789938764997}],"chatMessages":[],"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"deerflowEnabled":true,"outputs":[{"id":"out_gans_poster","workspaceId":"gans-goodfellow-2014","outputType":"poster","templateId":"betterposter","title":"Generative Adversarial Networks — Poster","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#1F2937","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_gan_p3","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Theoretical Guarantees & Convergence","column":2,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"When $D$ is trained to optimality for any given $G$ ($D^*_G(x) = \\\\frac{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x)}{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x) + p_g(x)}$), the minimax objective reduces to the Jensen-Shannon divergence:\\n\\n$$V(G, D^*_G) = -\\\\log 4 + 2 \\\\cdot D_{\\\\text{JS}}(p_{\\\\text{data}} \\\\parallel p_g)$$\\n\\nSince $D_{\\\\text{JS}}$ is always non-negative and zero only when distributions coincide, $G$ is guaranteed to perfectly replicate the data distribution under infinite capacity.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_gan_framework","url":"/api/workspaces/gans-goodfellow-2014/assets/fig_gan_framework.png","caption":"Figure 1: Generator vs Discriminator adversarial training cycle."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p4","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Benchmark Evaluation Results","column":3,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Parzen window-based log-likelihood estimates on MNIST and TFD:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Model Variant","MNIST Log-Likelihood","Toronto Face Database"],["DBN (Bengio et al.)","138 ± 2.0","1909 ± 66"],["Stacked CAE","121 ± 1.6","2110 ± 50"],["Deep GSN","214 ± 1.1","1890 ± 29"],["Adversarial Autoencoder","220 ± 1.8","2150 ± 42"],["GAN (Ours)","225 ± 2.0","2250 ± 60"]],"caption":"Table 1: Generative log-likelihood estimates.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p5","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Advantages, Limitations & Legacy","column":3,"order":1,"pattern":"references","content":"- **Advantages:** Computationally clean backpropagation without Markov chains; sharp, non-blurry sample generation.\\n- **Challenges:** Mode collapse and training instability; non-convergence in min-max game without proper regularization.\\n- **Legacy:** Sparked modern generative deep learning: DCGAN, CycleGAN, StyleGAN, and foundational concepts for latent diffusion.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p1","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Abstract & The Generative Challenge","column":1,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **The Generative Dilemma:** Prior generative models (RBMs, DBNs, VAEs) required intractable probabilistic computations (approximating partition functions) or Markov chain sampling.\\n- **Core Intuition:** Frame generative modeling as an adversarial game between two agents: a Generator $G$ and a Discriminator $D$ [@goodfellow2014generative].\\n- No Markov chains or unrolled approximate inference networks needed during training or generation.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p2","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"The Minimax Game Formulation","column":1,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"We train $D$ to maximize the probability of assigning the correct label to both training examples and generated samples, while simultaneously training $G$ to minimize $\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))$:\\n\\n$$\\\\min_G \\\\max_D V(D, G) = \\\\mathbb{E}_{x \\\\sim p_{\\\\text{data}}(x)}[\\\\log D(x)] + \\\\mathbb{E}_{z \\\\sim p_z(z)}[\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))]$$\\n\\n- **Global Optimum:** The minimax game achieves its unique global optimum if and only if $p_g = p_{\\\\text{data}}$, where $D(x) = 1/2$ everywhere.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_gans_paper","workspaceId":"gans-goodfellow-2014","outputType":"paper","templateId":"neurips","title":"Generative Adversarial Nets — Paper","isActive":true,"themeColor":"#111827","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_gan_a1","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"Abstract","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"section","content":"We propose a new framework for estimating generative models via an adversarial process, in which we simultaneously train two models: a generative model $G$ that captures the data distribution, and a discriminative model $D$ that estimates the probability that a sample came from the training data rather than $G$. The training procedure for $G$ is to maximize the probability of $D$ making a mistake. This framework corresponds to a minimax two-player game. In the space of arbitrary functions $G$ and $D$, a unique solution exists, with $G$ recovering the training data distribution and $D$ equal to $1/2$ everywhere. In the case where $G$ and $D$ are defined by multilayer perceptrons, the entire system can be trained with backpropagation. There is no need for any Markov chains or unrolled approximate inference networks during either training or generation of samples.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a2","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"1 Introduction","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"section","content":"The promise of deep learning is to discover rich, hierarchical models that represent probability distributions over the kinds of data encountered in artificial intelligence applications. So far, the most striking successes in deep learning have involved discriminative models, usually those that map a high-dimensional, rich sensory input to a class label. Deep generative models have had less of an impact, due to the difficulty of approximating many intractable probabilistic computations that arise in maximum likelihood estimation.\\\\n\\\\nWe propose a new generative model estimation procedure that sidesteps these difficulties. In the proposed adversarial nets framework, the generative model is pitted against an adversary: a discriminative model that learns to determine whether a sample is from the model distribution or the data distribution. The generative model can be thought of as analogous to a team of counterfeiters, trying to produce fake currency and use it without detection, while the discriminative model is analogous to the police, trying to detect the counterfeit currency.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a3","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"2 Adversarial Framework","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"section-figure","content":"The adversarial modeling framework is most straightforward to apply when the models are both multilayer perceptrons. To learn the generator''s distribution $p_g$ over data $x$, we define a prior on input noise variables $p_z(z)$, then represent a mapping to data space as $G(z; \\\\theta_g)$, where $G$ is a differentiable function represented by a multilayer perceptron with parameters $\\\\theta_g$. We also define a second multilayer perceptron $D(x; \\\\theta_d)$ that outputs a single scalar. $D(x)$ represents the probability that $x$ came from the data rather than $p_g$.\\\\n\\\\nWe train $D$ to maximize the probability of assigning the correct label to both training examples and samples from $G$. We simultaneously train $G$ to minimize $\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))$.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_gan_framework","url":"/api/workspaces/gans-goodfellow-2014/assets/fig_gan_framework.png","caption":"Figure 1: Generative adversarial net training dynamics."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a4","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"3 Experiments","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"section-table","content":"We trained adversarial nets on a range of datasets including MNIST, the Toronto Face Database (TFD), and CIFAR-10. The generator nets used a mixture of rectifier linear activations and sigmoid activations, while the discriminator net used maxout activations.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Model","MNIST Log-Likelihood","TFD Log-Likelihood"],["DBN","138 ± 2.0","1909 ± 66"],["Stacked CAE","121 ± 1.6","2110 ± 50"],["Deep GSN","214 ± 1.1","1890 ± 29"],["GAN (Ours)","225 ± 2.0","2250 ± 60"]],"caption":"Table 1: Parzen window-based log-likelihood estimates.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a5","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"4 Conclusion & References","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"references","content":"This framework admits many straightforward extensions: a conditional generative model $p(x \\\\mid c)$ can be obtained by feeding $c$ as input to both $G$ and $D$; learned approximate inference can be performed by training an auxiliary network to predict $z$ given $x$; and one can conditionally model all coordinates of $x$ given a subset of coordinates by training a family of conditional models that share parameters.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_gans_slides","workspaceId":"gans-goodfellow-2014","outputType":"slides","templateId":"beamer-focus","title":"Generative Adversarial Nets — Talk","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#1C1C1C","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_gan_s1","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"Title Slide","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"title-slide","content":"Generative Adversarial Nets\\\\n\\\\nIan J. Goodfellow, Jean Pouget-Abadie, Mehdi Mirza, Bing Xu\\\\nDavid Warde-Farley, Sherjil Ozair, Aaron Courville, Yoshua Bengio\\\\n\\\\nUniversité de Montréal\\\\nNeurIPS 2014","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Introduce the landmark GAN paper by Ian Goodfellow and Yoshua Bengio''s lab.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s2","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"The Generative Modeling Bottleneck","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- Discriminative deep learning had achieved huge breakthroughs (AlexNet, VGG).\\n- Generative models struggled due to intractable partition functions (RBMs, DBMs).\\n- Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling is slow and scales poorly in high dimensions.\\n- Key question: Can we generate samples using purely feedforward backpropagation?","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Highlight why generative modeling lagged behind discriminative vision in 2014.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s3","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"The Counterfeiter vs Police Game","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"two-column","content":"The Generator ($G$):\\n- Takes random noise $z \\\\sim p_z(z)$\\n- Synthesizes fake data $G(z)$\\n- Objective: Fool the discriminator\\n\\nThe Discriminator ($D$):\\n- Takes sample $x$ (real or fake)\\n- Outputs probability $D(x) \\\\in [0, 1]$\\n- Objective: Distinguish real from fake\\n\\nCompetition drives both networks to peak performance.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Explain the intuitive metaphor of the counterfeiter and the detective.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s4","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"The Minimax Formulation","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- Value function:\\n  $$\\\\min_G \\\\max_D V(D, G) = \\\\mathbb{E}_{x}[\\\\log D(x)] + \\\\mathbb{E}_{z}[\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))]$$\\n- For fixed $G$, optimal discriminator is $D^*_G(x) = \\\\frac{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x)}{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x) + p_g(x)}$.\\n- The game reaches equilibrium when $p_g = p_{\\\\text{data}}$ and $D(x) = 1/2$.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Explain the connection to Jensen-Shannon divergence and global optimality.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_gan_framework","url":"/api/workspaces/gans-goodfellow-2014/assets/fig_gan_framework.png","caption":"Figure: Adversarial training dynamics."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s5","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"Qualitative & Quantitative Results","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Demonstrated state-of-the-art sample fidelity without blurriness:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Point out that GAN samples were remarkably sharp compared to the blurry outputs typical of VAEs at the time.","table":{"rows":[["Method","MNIST","TFD"],["DBN","138 ± 2.0","1909 ± 66"],["Stacked CAE","121 ± 1.6","2110 ± 50"],["Deep GSN","214 ± 1.1","1890 ± 29"],["GAN (Ours)","225 ± 2.0","2250 ± 60"]],"caption":"Table: Log-likelihood estimates across benchmarks.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s6","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"Summary & The Generative Revolution","column":null,"order":5,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- Eliminated the need for Markov chains and approximate inference in generative models.\\n- Enabled realistic high-resolution image synthesis (DCGAN, StyleGAN).\\n- Established adversarial loss as a universal tool for domain adaptation and super-resolution.\\n- Fundamental conceptual milestone in generative artificial intelligence.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Conclude by highlighting how GANs sparked the explosion in generative AI research.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]}],"assets":[],"ingestFiles":[]}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "WorkspaceSnapshot" ("id", "workspaceId", "savedAt", "label", "source", "revision", "snapshot") VALUES ('cmuabc94e001q3i14udpwiqi5', 'gans-goodfellow-2014', '2026-09-20T21:13:09.423Z', 'Added new card: "Title Slide"', 'human', 2, '{"id":"gans-goodfellow-2014","name":"Generative Adversarial Nets","authors":"Ian J. Goodfellow, Jean Pouget-Abadie, Mehdi Mirza, Bing Xu, David Warde-Farley, Sherjil Ozair, Aaron Courville, Yoshua Bengio","venue":"NeurIPS 2014 (Université de Montréal)","userId":"user_3IGDYw03LkmHZaaCgKwWcBYxHQu","bibContent":"@inproceedings{goodfellow2014generative,\\n  title={Generative adversarial nets},\\n  author={Goodfellow, Ian and Pouget-Abadie, Jean and Mirza, Mehdi and Xu, Bing and Warde-Farley, David and Ozair, Sherjil and Courville, Aaron and Bengio, Yoshua},\\n  booktitle={Advances in neural information processing systems (NeurIPS)},\\n  volume={27},\\n  year={2014}\\n}\\n@inproceedings{salimans2016improved,\\n  title={Improved techniques for training gans},\\n  author={Salimans, Tim and Goodfellow, Ian and Zaremba, Wojciech and Cheung, Vicki and Radford, Alec and Chen, Xi},\\n  booktitle={NeurIPS},\\n  pages={2234--2242},\\n  year={2016}\\n}","revision":2,"bibKeys":null,"agentEvents":[{"id":"init","ts":"11:12:26 PM","kind":"info","title":"Editor ready","detail":"Loading workspace…","status":"done"},{"id":"24b4ee21-bd5a-4ce0-b8ce-91e3b721daad","ts":"11:12:27 PM","kind":"info","title":"Workspace loaded","detail":"5 cards · betterposter","status":"done","createdAt":1789938747194},{"id":"8f6a4fc8-dc7b-477d-9116-f047b05b6eb2","ts":"11:12:29 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938749878},{"id":"81b90f3f-d02f-42ce-9973-597eba195c13","ts":"11:12:37 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938757096},{"id":"705dd612-3a96-4bbe-98fe-32372136250e","ts":"11:12:44 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938764997},{"id":"dd118b1f-5b93-4213-b140-0db73b6c10db","ts":"11:12:52 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938772487},{"id":"7d99e717-50ee-4730-bf99-54db95ac4cff","ts":"11:13:06 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compiling poster with pdflatex…","detail":"Attempt 1/3: Saving workspace...","status":"running","createdAt":1789938786282}],"chatMessages":[],"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"deerflowEnabled":true,"outputs":[{"id":"out_gans_poster","workspaceId":"gans-goodfellow-2014","outputType":"poster","templateId":"betterposter","title":"Generative Adversarial Networks — Poster","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#1F2937","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_gan_p3","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Theoretical Guarantees & Convergence","column":2,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"When $D$ is trained to optimality for any given $G$ ($D^*_G(x) = \\\\frac{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x)}{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x) + p_g(x)}$), the minimax objective reduces to the Jensen-Shannon divergence:\\n\\n$$V(G, D^*_G) = -\\\\log 4 + 2 \\\\cdot D_{\\\\text{JS}}(p_{\\\\text{data}} \\\\parallel p_g)$$\\n\\nSince $D_{\\\\text{JS}}$ is always non-negative and zero only when distributions coincide, $G$ is guaranteed to perfectly replicate the data distribution under infinite capacity.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_gan_framework","url":"/api/workspaces/gans-goodfellow-2014/assets/fig_gan_framework.png","caption":"Figure 1: Generator vs Discriminator adversarial training cycle."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p4","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Benchmark Evaluation Results","column":3,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Parzen window-based log-likelihood estimates on MNIST and TFD:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Model Variant","MNIST Log-Likelihood","Toronto Face Database"],["DBN (Bengio et al.)","138 ± 2.0","1909 ± 66"],["Stacked CAE","121 ± 1.6","2110 ± 50"],["Deep GSN","214 ± 1.1","1890 ± 29"],["Adversarial Autoencoder","220 ± 1.8","2150 ± 42"],["GAN (Ours)","225 ± 2.0","2250 ± 60"]],"caption":"Table 1: Generative log-likelihood estimates.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p5","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Advantages, Limitations & Legacy","column":3,"order":1,"pattern":"references","content":"- **Advantages:** Computationally clean backpropagation without Markov chains; sharp, non-blurry sample generation.\\n- **Challenges:** Mode collapse and training instability; non-convergence in min-max game without proper regularization.\\n- **Legacy:** Sparked modern generative deep learning: DCGAN, CycleGAN, StyleGAN, and foundational concepts for latent diffusion.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p1","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"Abstract & The Generative Challenge","column":1,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **The Generative Dilemma:** Prior generative models (RBMs, DBNs, VAEs) required intractable probabilistic computations (approximating partition functions) or Markov chain sampling.\\n- **Core Intuition:** Frame generative modeling as an adversarial game between two agents: a Generator $G$ and a Discriminator $D$ [@goodfellow2014generative].\\n- No Markov chains or unrolled approximate inference networks needed during training or generation.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_p2","outputId":"out_gans_poster","title":"The Minimax Game Formulation","column":1,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"We train $D$ to maximize the probability of assigning the correct label to both training examples and generated samples, while simultaneously training $G$ to minimize $\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))$:\\n\\n$$\\\\min_G \\\\max_D V(D, G) = \\\\mathbb{E}_{x \\\\sim p_{\\\\text{data}}(x)}[\\\\log D(x)] + \\\\mathbb{E}_{z \\\\sim p_z(z)}[\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))]$$\\n\\n- **Global Optimum:** The minimax game achieves its unique global optimum if and only if $p_g = p_{\\\\text{data}}$, where $D(x) = 1/2$ everywhere.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_gans_paper","workspaceId":"gans-goodfellow-2014","outputType":"paper","templateId":"neurips","title":"Generative Adversarial Nets — Paper","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#111827","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_gan_a1","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"Abstract","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"section","content":"We propose a new framework for estimating generative models via an adversarial process, in which we simultaneously train two models: a generative model $G$ that captures the data distribution, and a discriminative model $D$ that estimates the probability that a sample came from the training data rather than $G$. The training procedure for $G$ is to maximize the probability of $D$ making a mistake. This framework corresponds to a minimax two-player game. In the space of arbitrary functions $G$ and $D$, a unique solution exists, with $G$ recovering the training data distribution and $D$ equal to $1/2$ everywhere. In the case where $G$ and $D$ are defined by multilayer perceptrons, the entire system can be trained with backpropagation. There is no need for any Markov chains or unrolled approximate inference networks during either training or generation of samples.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a2","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"1 Introduction","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"section","content":"The promise of deep learning is to discover rich, hierarchical models that represent probability distributions over the kinds of data encountered in artificial intelligence applications. So far, the most striking successes in deep learning have involved discriminative models, usually those that map a high-dimensional, rich sensory input to a class label. Deep generative models have had less of an impact, due to the difficulty of approximating many intractable probabilistic computations that arise in maximum likelihood estimation.\\\\n\\\\nWe propose a new generative model estimation procedure that sidesteps these difficulties. In the proposed adversarial nets framework, the generative model is pitted against an adversary: a discriminative model that learns to determine whether a sample is from the model distribution or the data distribution. The generative model can be thought of as analogous to a team of counterfeiters, trying to produce fake currency and use it without detection, while the discriminative model is analogous to the police, trying to detect the counterfeit currency.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a3","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"2 Adversarial Framework","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"section-figure","content":"The adversarial modeling framework is most straightforward to apply when the models are both multilayer perceptrons. To learn the generator''s distribution $p_g$ over data $x$, we define a prior on input noise variables $p_z(z)$, then represent a mapping to data space as $G(z; \\\\theta_g)$, where $G$ is a differentiable function represented by a multilayer perceptron with parameters $\\\\theta_g$. We also define a second multilayer perceptron $D(x; \\\\theta_d)$ that outputs a single scalar. $D(x)$ represents the probability that $x$ came from the data rather than $p_g$.\\\\n\\\\nWe train $D$ to maximize the probability of assigning the correct label to both training examples and samples from $G$. We simultaneously train $G$ to minimize $\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))$.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_gan_framework","url":"/api/workspaces/gans-goodfellow-2014/assets/fig_gan_framework.png","caption":"Figure 1: Generative adversarial net training dynamics."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a4","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"3 Experiments","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"section-table","content":"We trained adversarial nets on a range of datasets including MNIST, the Toronto Face Database (TFD), and CIFAR-10. The generator nets used a mixture of rectifier linear activations and sigmoid activations, while the discriminator net used maxout activations.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Model","MNIST Log-Likelihood","TFD Log-Likelihood"],["DBN","138 ± 2.0","1909 ± 66"],["Stacked CAE","121 ± 1.6","2110 ± 50"],["Deep GSN","214 ± 1.1","1890 ± 29"],["GAN (Ours)","225 ± 2.0","2250 ± 60"]],"caption":"Table 1: Parzen window-based log-likelihood estimates.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_a5","outputId":"out_gans_paper","title":"4 Conclusion & References","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"references","content":"This framework admits many straightforward extensions: a conditional generative model $p(x \\\\mid c)$ can be obtained by feeding $c$ as input to both $G$ and $D$; learned approximate inference can be performed by training an auxiliary network to predict $z$ given $x$; and one can conditionally model all coordinates of $x$ given a subset of coordinates by training a family of conditional models that share parameters.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_gans_slides","workspaceId":"gans-goodfellow-2014","outputType":"slides","templateId":"beamer-focus","title":"Generative Adversarial Nets — Talk","isActive":true,"themeColor":"#1C1C1C","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_gan_s1","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"Title Slide","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"title-slide","content":"Generative Adversarial Nets\\\\n\\\\nIan J. Goodfellow, Jean Pouget-Abadie, Mehdi Mirza, Bing Xu\\\\nDavid Warde-Farley, Sherjil Ozair, Aaron Courville, Yoshua Bengio\\\\n\\\\nUniversité de Montréal\\\\nNeurIPS 2014","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Introduce the landmark GAN paper by Ian Goodfellow and Yoshua Bengio''s lab.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s2","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"The Generative Modeling Bottleneck","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- Discriminative deep learning had achieved huge breakthroughs (AlexNet, VGG).\\n- Generative models struggled due to intractable partition functions (RBMs, DBMs).\\n- Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling is slow and scales poorly in high dimensions.\\n- Key question: Can we generate samples using purely feedforward backpropagation?","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Highlight why generative modeling lagged behind discriminative vision in 2014.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s3","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"The Counterfeiter vs Police Game","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"two-column","content":"The Generator ($G$):\\n- Takes random noise $z \\\\sim p_z(z)$\\n- Synthesizes fake data $G(z)$\\n- Objective: Fool the discriminator\\n\\nThe Discriminator ($D$):\\n- Takes sample $x$ (real or fake)\\n- Outputs probability $D(x) \\\\in [0, 1]$\\n- Objective: Distinguish real from fake\\n\\nCompetition drives both networks to peak performance.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Explain the intuitive metaphor of the counterfeiter and the detective.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s4","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"The Minimax Formulation","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- Value function:\\n  $$\\\\min_G \\\\max_D V(D, G) = \\\\mathbb{E}_{x}[\\\\log D(x)] + \\\\mathbb{E}_{z}[\\\\log(1 - D(G(z)))]$$\\n- For fixed $G$, optimal discriminator is $D^*_G(x) = \\\\frac{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x)}{p_{\\\\text{data}}(x) + p_g(x)}$.\\n- The game reaches equilibrium when $p_g = p_{\\\\text{data}}$ and $D(x) = 1/2$.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Explain the connection to Jensen-Shannon divergence and global optimality.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_gan_framework","url":"/api/workspaces/gans-goodfellow-2014/assets/fig_gan_framework.png","caption":"Figure: Adversarial training dynamics."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s5","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"Qualitative & Quantitative Results","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Demonstrated state-of-the-art sample fidelity without blurriness:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Point out that GAN samples were remarkably sharp compared to the blurry outputs typical of VAEs at the time.","table":{"rows":[["Method","MNIST","TFD"],["DBN","138 ± 2.0","1909 ± 66"],["Stacked CAE","121 ± 1.6","2110 ± 50"],["Deep GSN","214 ± 1.1","1890 ± 29"],["GAN (Ours)","225 ± 2.0","2250 ± 60"]],"caption":"Table: Log-likelihood estimates across benchmarks.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_gan_s6","outputId":"out_gans_slides","title":"Summary & The Generative Revolution","column":null,"order":5,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- Eliminated the need for Markov chains and approximate inference in generative models.\\n- Enabled realistic high-resolution image synthesis (DCGAN, StyleGAN).\\n- Established adversarial loss as a universal tool for domain adaptation and super-resolution.\\n- Fundamental conceptual milestone in generative artificial intelligence.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Conclude by highlighting how GANs sparked the explosion in generative AI research.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]}],"assets":[],"ingestFiles":[]}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "WorkspaceSnapshot" ("id", "workspaceId", "savedAt", "label", "source", "revision", "snapshot") VALUES ('cmuablstv001t3i14qluwlmq3', 'resnet-deep-residual-learning', '2026-09-20T21:20:34.867Z', 'Created initial project layout.', 'human', 1, '{"id":"resnet-deep-residual-learning","name":"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition","authors":"Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun","venue":"CVPR 2016 (Microsoft Research) — Best Paper Award","userId":"user_3IGDYw03LkmHZaaCgKwWcBYxHQu","bibContent":"@inproceedings{he2016deep,\\n  title={Deep residual learning for image recognition},\\n  author={He, Kaiming and Zhang, Xiangyu and Ren, Shaoqing and Sun, Jian},\\n  booktitle={Proceedings of the IEEE conference on computer vision and pattern recognition (CVPR)},\\n  pages={770--778},\\n  year={2016}\\n}\\n@inproceedings{simonyan2014very,\\n  title={Very deep convolutional networks for large-scale image recognition},\\n  author={Simonyan, Karen and Zisserman, Andrew},\\n  booktitle={International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\\n  year={2015}\\n}","revision":1,"bibKeys":null,"agentEvents":[{"id":"init","ts":"11:12:26 PM","kind":"info","title":"Editor ready","detail":"Loading workspace…","status":"done"},{"id":"24b4ee21-bd5a-4ce0-b8ce-91e3b721daad","ts":"11:12:27 PM","kind":"info","title":"Workspace loaded","detail":"5 cards · betterposter","status":"done","createdAt":1789938747194},{"id":"8f6a4fc8-dc7b-477d-9116-f047b05b6eb2","ts":"11:12:29 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938749878},{"id":"81b90f3f-d02f-42ce-9973-597eba195c13","ts":"11:12:37 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938757096},{"id":"705dd612-3a96-4bbe-98fe-32372136250e","ts":"11:12:44 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938764997},{"id":"dd118b1f-5b93-4213-b140-0db73b6c10db","ts":"11:12:52 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938772487},{"id":"7d99e717-50ee-4730-bf99-54db95ac4cff","ts":"11:13:06 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938786282},{"id":"c211d435-f11c-4b3f-a663-e1e5761768bc","ts":"11:13:13 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938793799},{"id":"4da43978-20ae-48f5-889f-606e716a0cd8","ts":"11:13:35 PM","kind":"info","title":"Workspace loaded","detail":"7 cards · atlas","status":"done","createdAt":1789938815213},{"id":"a49a6253-b732-4db4-8868-ad3b868dc46a","ts":"11:13:37 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938817830},{"id":"4a14bf5c-5c40-4b0a-b2a6-4c1cd1ad4fb6","ts":"11:13:47 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938827732},{"id":"8f3260da-72c4-4902-8267-311bbda28fd0","ts":"11:20:32 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compiling poster with pdflatex…","detail":"Attempt 1/3: Saving workspace...","status":"running","createdAt":1789939232620}],"chatMessages":[],"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"deerflowEnabled":true,"outputs":[{"id":"out_resnet_poster","workspaceId":"resnet-deep-residual-learning","outputType":"poster","templateId":"atlas","title":"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition — Poster","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#C8102E","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_res_p6","outputId":"out_resnet_poster","title":"Generalization Across Competitions","column":3,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"ResNet won 1st place across all 5 tracks at ILSVRC & COCO 2015 competitions:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Competition Track","ResNet Performance","Margin / Baseline"],["ImageNet Classification","3.57% top-5 error","Surpassed human vision"],["ImageNet Detection","62.1% mAP","+16% absolute gain"],["ImageNet Localization","9.0% error","1st place"],["COCO Detection","37.4% AP","+28% relative gain"],["COCO Segmentation","32.2% AP","+21% relative gain"]],"caption":"Table 2: 1st place benchmark sweep across vision challenges.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_p1","outputId":"out_resnet_poster","title":"Abstract & Degradation Mystery","column":1,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **Depth Paradox:** Deep convolutional networks naturally integrate low/mid/high level features. Is learning better models as simple as stacking more layers?\\n- **The Degradation Problem:** When deeper networks converge, accuracy saturates and degrades rapidly. This degradation is **not caused by overfitting** — training error itself worsens with more layers!\\n- **Key Hypothesis:** Fitting an identity mapping $H(x) = x$ using multiple stacked nonlinear layers is counter-intuitively difficult [@he2016deep].","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_p2","outputId":"out_resnet_poster","title":"Residual Formulation","column":1,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"Instead of hoping stacked layers fit $\\\\mathcal{H}(x)$, we let them approximate a residual mapping $\\\\mathcal{F}(x) := \\\\mathcal{H}(x) - x$:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal{H}(x) = \\\\mathcal{F}(x, \\\\{W_i\\\\}) + x$$\\n\\n- **Identity Shortcut Connections:** Realized by parameter-free skip connections performing identity mapping.\\n- **Zero Computational Overhead:** Identity shortcuts add no extra parameters and require zero additional FLOPs during inference.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_p3","outputId":"out_resnet_poster","title":"Residual Learning Block","column":2,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- **Basic Block:** Two stacked $3 \\\\times 3$ conv layers with Batch Normalization and ReLU, bypassed by identity shortcut $x$.\\n- **Bottleneck Design:** For deep networks (ResNet-50/101/152), stack $1 \\\\times 1$ (dim reduction), $3 \\\\times 3$, and $1 \\\\times 1$ (dim restoration) convs.\\n- Enables training 152 layers with lower FLOPs than VGG-19.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_residual_block","url":"/api/workspaces/resnet-deep-residual-learning/assets/fig_residual_block.png","caption":"Figure 1: Residual building block with identity skip connection."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_p4","outputId":"out_resnet_poster","title":"Optimization Dynamics","column":2,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- **Plain Networks:** 34-layer plain net has higher training error than 18-layer plain net throughout optimization.\\n- **ResNet Networks:** 34-layer ResNet achieves considerably lower training error and generalizes better than 18-layer ResNet.\\n- Residual connections eliminate vanishing gradients across deep layer stacks.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_convergence_curves","url":"/api/workspaces/resnet-deep-residual-learning/assets/fig_convergence_curves.png","caption":"Figure 2: Training and validation error on ImageNet comparing plain vs residual networks."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_p5","outputId":"out_resnet_poster","title":"ImageNet Classification Leaderboard","column":3,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Evaluation on ImageNet 2015 validation set:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Architecture","Top-1 Error","Top-5 Error","Layers"],["VGG-16","28.07%","9.33%","16"],["GoogLeNet","—","9.15%","22"],["ResNet-34 (plain)","28.54%","10.02%","34"],["ResNet-34 (residual)","25.03%","7.76%","34"],["ResNet-50","22.85%","6.71%","50"],["ResNet-101","21.75%","6.05%","101"],["ResNet-152 (ensemble)","19.38%","3.57%","152"]],"caption":"Table 1: Error rates on ImageNet classification.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_p7","outputId":"out_resnet_poster","title":"References & Impact","column":3,"order":2,"pattern":"references","content":"- **Winner of CVPR 2016 Best Paper Award.**\\\\n- Demonstrated networks can scale to 152 layers on ImageNet and 1202 layers on CIFAR-10.\\\\n- Residual connections are now universal across ConvNets, Transformers, AlphaGo, and diffusion models.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_resnet_paper","workspaceId":"resnet-deep-residual-learning","outputType":"paper","templateId":"article-twocol","title":"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition — Paper","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#111827","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_res_a1","outputId":"out_resnet_paper","title":"Abstract","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"section","content":"Deeper neural networks are more difficult to train. We present a residual learning framework to ease the training of networks that are substantially deeper than those used previously. We explicitly reformulate the layers as learning residual functions with reference to the layer inputs, instead of learning unreferenced functions. We provide comprehensive empirical evidence showing that these residual networks are easier to optimize, and can gain accuracy from considerably increased depth. On the ImageNet dataset we evaluate residual nets with a depth of up to 152 layers—8× deeper than VGG nets but still having lower complexity. An ensemble of these residual nets achieves 3.57% error on the ImageNet test set. This result won the 1st place on the ILSVRC 2015 classification task.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_a2","outputId":"out_resnet_paper","title":"1 Introduction","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"section","content":"Deep convolutional neural networks have led to a series of breakthroughs for image classification. Driven by the significance of depth, a question arises: Is learning better networks as easy as stacking more layers? When deeper networks start converging, a degradation problem has been exposed: with network depth increasing, accuracy becomes saturated and then degrades rapidly. Unexpectedly, such degradation is not caused by overfitting, and adding more layers to a suitably deep model leads to higher training error.\\\\n\\\\nWe present a deep residual learning framework. Instead of hoping every few stacked layers directly fit a desired underlying mapping, we explicitly let these layers fit a residual mapping.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_a3","outputId":"out_resnet_paper","title":"2 Deep Residual Learning Framework","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"section-figure","content":"Let us consider $\\\\mathcal{H}(x)$ as an underlying mapping to be fit by a few stacked layers, with $x$ denoting the inputs to the first of these layers. Rather than expect stacked layers to approximate $\\\\mathcal{H}(x)$, we explicitly let these layers approximate a residual function $\\\\mathcal{F}(x) := \\\\mathcal{H}(x) - x$. The original mapping is recast into $\\\\mathcal{F}(x) + x$.\\\\n\\\\nWe hypothesize that it is easier to optimize the residual mapping than to optimize the original, unreferenced mapping. To the extreme, if an identity mapping were optimal, it would be easier to push the residual to zero than to fit an identity mapping by a stack of nonlinear layers.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_residual_block","url":"/api/workspaces/resnet-deep-residual-learning/assets/fig_residual_block.png","caption":"Figure 1: Residual learning building block."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_a4","outputId":"out_resnet_paper","title":"3 Experiments on ImageNet","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"section-table","content":"We evaluate our method on the ImageNet 2012 classification dataset that consists of 1000 classes. The models are trained on 1.28 million training images, and evaluated on the 50k validation images.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Model","Top-1 Error","Top-5 Error"],["VGG-16","28.07%","9.33%"],["GoogLeNet","—","9.15%"],["ResNet-18","27.88%","8.90%"],["ResNet-34","25.03%","7.76%"],["ResNet-50","22.85%","6.71%"],["ResNet-101","21.75%","6.05%"],["ResNet-152","21.43%","5.71%"]],"caption":"Table 1: Top-1 and Top-5 error rates on ImageNet validation.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_a5","outputId":"out_resnet_paper","title":"4 Conclusion & References","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"references","content":"We have presented deep residual networks for image recognition. Our residual framework addresses the degradation problem and allows training networks with depth up to 152 layers on ImageNet and over 1000 layers on CIFAR-10. Our models won 1st place in all five tracks of the ILSVRC and COCO 2015 competitions.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_resnet_slides","workspaceId":"resnet-deep-residual-learning","outputType":"slides","templateId":"beamer-atlas","title":"Deep Residual Learning — Conference Slides","isActive":true,"themeColor":"#C8102E","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_res_s2","outputId":"out_resnet_slides","title":"The Mystery of Degradation","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- Deeper neural networks naturally extract richer hierarchical representations.\\n- But stacking more layers leads to a surprising **degradation problem**.\\n- 56-layer plain network has **higher training error** than 20-layer network.\\n- It is not overfitting: the optimization problem is fundamentally harder.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Emphasize that the degradation problem was not overfitting because training error was worse on deeper models.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_s3","outputId":"out_resnet_slides","title":"The Residual Learning Solution","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"two-column","content":"Original Goal:\\n- Fit underlying mapping $\\\\mathcal{H}(x)$\\n\\nResidual Reformulation:\\n- Let layers fit $\\\\mathcal{F}(x) = \\\\mathcal{H}(x) - x$\\n- Final output: $\\\\mathcal{H}(x) = \\\\mathcal{F}(x) + x$\\n\\nWhy this works:\\n- If identity mapping is optimal, driving weights to zero is much easier than fitting identity mapping with non-linear activations.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Explain the mathematical intuition behind residual learning and shortcut connections.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_s6","outputId":"out_resnet_slides","title":"Summary & Takeaways","column":null,"order":5,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- Residual learning resolves the deep network degradation problem.\\n- Enables training of arbitrarily deep networks (152 layers on ImageNet, 1202 on CIFAR).\\n- Zero parameter overhead for identity shortcuts.\\n- Established standard architecture across computer vision and multimodal AI.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Conclude by highlighting ResNet''s enduring influence across all areas of modern deep learning.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_s1","outputId":"out_resnet_slides","title":"Title Slide","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"title-slide","content":"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition\\\\n\\\\nKaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun\\\\n\\\\nMicrosoft Research\\\\nCVPR 2016","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Introduce the seminal work on ResNets that won Best Paper at CVPR 2016.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_s4","outputId":"out_resnet_slides","title":"Residual Block Architectures","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- **Basic Block (ResNet-18/34):** Two $3 \\\\times 3$ convs with identity shortcut.\\n- **Bottleneck Block (ResNet-50/101/152):** $1 \\\\times 1$ conv (reduction) $\\\\to$ $3 \\\\times 3$ conv $\\\\to$ $1 \\\\times 1$ conv (expansion).\\n- Bottleneck reduces parameter count and FLOPs, allowing up to 152 layers.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Walk through the bottleneck design that made 152 layers computationally practical.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_residual_block","url":"/api/workspaces/resnet-deep-residual-learning/assets/fig_residual_block.png","caption":"Figure: Basic and bottleneck residual blocks."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_res_s5","outputId":"out_resnet_slides","title":"ImageNet 2015 Results","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"ResNet swept 1st place across competition tracks:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Highlight the 3.57% top-5 error, which was the first AI system to beat estimated human performance on ImageNet.","table":{"rows":[["Model","Top-5 Error","Depth"],["VGG-16","9.33%","16 layers"],["GoogLeNet","9.15%","22 layers"],["ResNet-34","7.76%","34 layers"],["ResNet-152 (ensemble)","3.57%","152 layers"]],"caption":"Table: ImageNet 2015 classification error.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]}],"assets":[],"ingestFiles":[]}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "WorkspaceSnapshot" ("id", "workspaceId", "savedAt", "label", "source", "revision", "snapshot") VALUES ('cmuabrwds001w3i1401il62qk', 'bert-pre-training', '2026-09-20T21:25:19.408Z', 'Created initial project layout.', 'human', 1, '{"id":"bert-pre-training","name":"BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers","authors":"Jacob Devlin, Ming-Wei Chang, Kenton Lee, Kristina Toutanova","venue":"NAACL 2019 (Google AI Language) — Best Long Paper Award","userId":"user_3IGDYw03LkmHZaaCgKwWcBYxHQu","bibContent":"@inproceedings{devlin2019bert,\\n  title={BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding},\\n  author={Devlin, Jacob and Chang, Ming-Wei and Lee, Kenton and Toutanova, Kristina},\\n  booktitle={Proceedings of NAACL-HLT},\\n  pages={4171--4186},\\n  year={2019}\\n}\\n@article{radford2018improving,\\n  title={Improving language understanding by generative pre-training},\\n  author={Radford, Alec and Narasimhan, Karthik and Salimans, Tim and Sutskever, Ilya},\\n  year={2018}\\n}","revision":1,"bibKeys":null,"agentEvents":[{"id":"init","ts":"11:12:26 PM","kind":"info","title":"Editor ready","detail":"Loading workspace…","status":"done"},{"id":"24b4ee21-bd5a-4ce0-b8ce-91e3b721daad","ts":"11:12:27 PM","kind":"info","title":"Workspace loaded","detail":"5 cards · betterposter","status":"done","createdAt":1789938747194},{"id":"8f6a4fc8-dc7b-477d-9116-f047b05b6eb2","ts":"11:12:29 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789938749878},{"id":"81b90f3f-d02f-42ce-9973-597eba195c13","ts":"11:12:37 PM","kind":"info","title":"VLM Layout Check failed","detail":"Error: HTTP 500: {\\"error\\":\\"AI provider circuit is open for http://127.0.0.1:8045/v1/chat/completions (recent consecutive failures); skipping\\"}","status":"error","createdAt":1789938757096},{"id":"705dd612-3a96-4bbe-98fe-32372136250e","ts":"11:12:44 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded",