null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Model Variant","SQuAD 1.1 EM","SQuAD 1.1 F1","SQuAD 2.0 F1"],["BiDAF + Self-Attn","77.8","84.1","65.6"],["BERT-Base","80.8","88.5","76.3"],["BERT-Large","84.1","90.9","81.9"],["Human Performance","82.3","91.2","86.8"]],"caption":"Table 2: SQuAD benchmark performance comparing to human baseline.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_p4","outputId":"out_bert_poster","title":"Model Architecture & Pre-training","column":2,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- **Transformer Encoder Backbone:** Multi-layer bidirectional Transformer encoder based on original Vaswani et al. architecture.\\n- **BERT-Base:** $L=12$ layers, $H=768$ hidden size, $A=12$ attention heads (110M parameters).\\n- **BERT-Large:** $L=24$ layers, $H=1024$ hidden size, $A=16$ attention heads (340M parameters).\\n- Trained on BooksCorpus (800M words) + English Wikipedia (2,500M words).","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_bert_arch","url":"/api/workspaces/bert-pre-training/assets/fig_bert_arch.png","caption":"Figure 1: Pre-training and fine-tuning procedures for BERT."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_p5","outputId":"out_bert_poster","title":"Fine-Tuning Paradigm","column":2,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- **Task-Agnostic Pre-training:** Same pre-trained parameters initialized for distinct downstream tasks.\\n- **Minimal Task-Specific Layers:** Add one output layer for classification or QA token spans.\\n- **End-to-End Fine-Tuning:** All parameters fine-tuned together with modest compute (1–4 GPU hours per task).","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_attention_map","url":"/api/workspaces/bert-pre-training/assets/fig_attention_map.png","caption":"Figure 2: Attention distributions across BERT encoder layers."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_p6","outputId":"out_bert_poster","title":"GLUE Benchmark Results","column":3,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Evaluation on the General Language Understanding Evaluation (GLUE) benchmark:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["System","MNLI (m/mm)","QQP","QNLI","SST-2","CoLA","GLUE Score"],["Pre-OpenAI SOTA","80.6 / 80.1","66.1","82.3","90.4","35.0","74.0"],["OpenAI GPT","82.1 / 81.4","70.3","87.4","91.3","45.4","75.1"],["BERT-Base","84.6 / 83.4","71.2","90.5","93.5","52.1","79.6"],["BERT-Large","86.7 / 85.9","72.1","92.7","94.9","60.5","82.1"]],"caption":"Table 1: GLUE test results scored by the official GLUE server.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_p8","outputId":"out_bert_poster","title":"References & Historic Impact","column":3,"order":2,"pattern":"references","content":"- **Winner of NAACL 2019 Best Long Paper Award.**\\\\n- Established the pre-train then fine-tune paradigm as the universal default in NLP.\\\\n- Directly powered Google Search core ranking algorithms worldwide from 2019 onwards.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_p1","outputId":"out_bert_poster","title":"Abstract & Left-to-Right Limitation","column":1,"order":0,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- **Unidirectional Bottleneck:** Prior language models (OpenAI GPT, ELMo) used left-to-right architectures where tokens only attend to previous context.\\n- **Sub-optimal for Sentence-Level Tasks:** In question answering and sentence pair classification, understanding context from **both directions simultaneously** is crucial.\\n- **BERT Solution:** Pre-train deep bidirectional representations by conditioning on both left and right context in all Transformer layers [@devlin2019bert].","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_p2","outputId":"out_bert_poster","title":"Masked Language Model (MLM)","column":1,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"To train bidirectionality without allowing tokens to indirectly ''see themselves'' in deep attention, we use the Cloze objective:\\n\\n- Randomly mask 15% of all input tokens.\\n- Of masked tokens: 80% replaced with [MASK], 10% replaced with random token, 10% left unchanged.\\n- Cross-entropy loss computed exclusively over masked token predictions:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal{L}_{\\\\text{MLM}}(\\\\theta) = -\\\\sum_{i \\\\in \\\\text{masked}} \\\\log P(x_i \\\\mid \\\\tilde{X}; \\\\theta)$$","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_p3","outputId":"out_bert_poster","title":"Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)","column":1,"order":2,"pattern":"bullets","content":"To learn relationships between sentence pairs for QA and Natural Language Inference (NLI):\\n\\n- Input: Pair of sentences $A$ and $B$, separated by [SEP].\\n- 50% of the time, $B$ is the actual next sentence that follows $A$ (IsNext).\\n- 50% of the time, $B$ is a random sentence from the corpus (NotNext).\\n- Final hidden vector of the first token [CLS] used for binary classification.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_bert_paper","workspaceId":"bert-pre-training","outputType":"paper","templateId":"neurips","title":"BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers — Paper","isActive":false,"themeColor":"#111827","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_bert_a5","outputId":"out_bert_paper","title":"4 Conclusion & References","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"references","content":"Recent empirical improvements due to transfer learning with language models have demonstrated that rich, unsupervised pre-training is an integral part of many language understanding systems. In particular, these results enable even low-resource tasks to benefit from deep unidirectional architectures. Our major contribution is further generalizing these findings to deep bidirectional architectures, allowing the same pre-trained model to successfully tackle a broad set of NLP tasks.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_a4","outputId":"out_bert_paper","title":"3 Experiments on GLUE","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"section-table","content":"The General Language Understanding Evaluation (GLUE) benchmark is a collection of diverse natural language understanding tasks. To fine-tune on GLUE, we represent the input sequence and use the first token [CLS] representation as the aggregate representation.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[["Model","MNLI-(m/mm)","QQP","QNLI","SST-2","CoLA","Average"],["OpenAI GPT","82.1 / 81.4","70.3","87.4","91.3","45.4","75.1"],["BERT-Base","84.6 / 83.4","71.2","90.5","93.5","52.1","79.6"],["BERT-Large","86.7 / 85.9","72.1","92.7","94.9","60.5","82.1"]],"caption":"Table 1: GLUE test results. State-of-the-art across all benchmarks.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_a1","outputId":"out_bert_paper","title":"Abstract","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"section","content":"We introduce a new language representation model called BERT, which stands for Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers. Unlike recent language representation models, BERT is designed to pre-train deep bidirectional representations from unlabeled text by jointly conditioning on both left and right context in all layers. As a result, the pre-trained BERT model can be fine-tuned with just one additional output layer to create state-of-the-art models for a wide range of tasks, such as question answering and language inference, without substantial task-specific architecture modifications. BERT is conceptually simple and empirically powerful. It obtains new state-of-the-art results on eleven natural language processing tasks, including pushing the GLUE score to 82.1% (7.7% point absolute improvement), MultiNLI accuracy to 86.7%, and SQuAD v1.1 question answering Test F1 to 93.2.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_a2","outputId":"out_bert_paper","title":"1 Introduction","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"section","content":"Language model pre-training has been shown to be effective for improving many natural language processing tasks. There are two existing strategies for applying pre-trained language representations to downstream tasks: feature-based and fine-tuning. The feature-based approach, such as ELMo, uses task-specific architectures that include the pre-trained representations as additional features. The fine-tuning approach, such as the Generative Pre-trained Transformer (OpenAI GPT), introduces minimal task-specific parameters, and is trained on the downstream tasks by simply fine-tuning all pre-trained parameters.\\\\n\\\\nWe argue that current techniques restrict the power of the pre-trained representations. The major limitation is that standard language models are unidirectional, and this limits the choice of architectures that can be used during pre-training.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_a3","outputId":"out_bert_paper","title":"2 BERT Architecture & Pre-training","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"section-figure","content":"There are two steps in our framework: pre-training and fine-tuning. During pre-training, the model is trained on unlabeled data over different pre-training tasks. For fine-tuning, the BERT model is first initialized with the pre-trained parameters, and all of the parameters are fine-tuned using labeled data from the downstream tasks.\\\\n\\\\nBERT''s model architecture is a multi-layer bidirectional Transformer encoder based on the original implementation described in Vaswani et al. We denote the number of layers as $L$, the hidden size as $H$, and the number of self-attention heads as $A$. We primarily report results on two model sizes: BERT-Base ($L=12, H=768, A=12$, 110M params) and BERT-Large ($L=24, H=1024, A=16$, 340M params).","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":null,"table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_bert_arch","url":"/api/workspaces/bert-pre-training/assets/fig_bert_arch.png","caption":"Figure 1: Differences in pre-training model architectures."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]},{"id":"out_bert_slides","workspaceId":"bert-pre-training","outputType":"slides","templateId":"beamer-madrid","title":"BERT Explained — Conference Slides","isActive":true,"themeColor":"#1D4ED8","authors":null,"sourceIds":[],"venue":null,"logoUrl":null,"secondaryLogoUrl":null,"cards":[{"id":"card_bert_s2","outputId":"out_bert_slides","title":"The Unidirectional Limitation","column":null,"order":1,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- OpenAI GPT was strictly left-to-right: each token only attends to past context.\\n- ELMo concatenated independent forward and backward LSTMs without joint conditioning.\\n- In QA (e.g. SQuAD), reading both left and right context simultaneously is essential.\\n- Challenge: In deep self-attention, bidirectional conditioning allows words to indirectly ''see themselves''.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Emphasize why simple bidirectional self-attention is impossible without masking.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_s1","outputId":"out_bert_slides","title":"Title Slide","column":null,"order":0,"pattern":"title-slide","content":"BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding\\\\n\\\\nJacob Devlin, Ming-Wei Chang, Kenton Lee, Kristina Toutanova\\\\n\\\\nGoogle AI Language\\\\nNAACL 2019","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Introduce BERT and explain why it represented a major paradigm shift in NLP.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_s3","outputId":"out_bert_slides","title":"Masked Language Model (MLM)","column":null,"order":2,"pattern":"two-column","content":"The Cloze Task:\\n- Mask 15% of input tokens at random.\\n- Model must predict the masked tokens using bidirectional Transformer context.\\n\\nMasking Recipe:\\n- 80% replaced with [MASK] token\\n- 10% replaced with random word\\n- 10% kept unchanged\\n\\nCross-entropy loss only penalizes predictions on masked positions.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Walk through the 80/10/10 masking recipe and explain why it prevents mismatch between pre-training and fine-tuning.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_s4","outputId":"out_bert_slides","title":"Next Sentence Prediction (NSP)","column":null,"order":3,"pattern":"bullets-image","content":"- Pre-trains the model to understand sentence relationships for QA and NLI.\\n- Input: [CLS] Sentence A [SEP] Sentence B [SEP].\\n- 50% positive pairs (IsNext), 50% random pairs (NotNext).\\n- [CLS] token embedding feeds into binary classifier.","figureLayout":"single","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Explain that NSP enabled BERT to excel at sentence pair tasks like NLI and question answering.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[{"id":"fig_bert_arch","url":"/api/workspaces/bert-pre-training/assets/fig_bert_arch.png","caption":"Figure: BERT pre-training architecture."}],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_s5","outputId":"out_bert_slides","title":"GLUE & SQuAD SOTA Sweeps","column":null,"order":4,"pattern":"bullets-table","content":"Massive gains across all evaluation benchmarks:","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Point out that BERT-Large became the first system to achieve superhuman performance on SQuAD 1.1.","table":{"rows":[["Benchmark","Prior SOTA","BERT-Base","BERT-Large"],["GLUE Average","74.0%","79.6%","82.1% (+8.1)"],["MNLI (accuracy)","82.1%","84.6%","86.7%"],["SQuAD 1.1 F1","91.2 (human)","88.5","93.2 (superhuman)"],["SQuAD 2.0 F1","86.8 (human)","76.3","81.9"]],"caption":"Table: Benchmark comparisons across NLP tasks.","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null},{"id":"card_bert_s6","outputId":"out_bert_slides","title":"Summary & Takeaways","column":null,"order":5,"pattern":"bullets","content":"- Deep bidirectionality is essential for full language understanding.\\n- MLM + NSP allows robust self-supervised pre-training on unlabeled text.\\n- Pre-train once, fine-tune everywhere with minimal task-specific parameters.\\n- Ushered in the modern era of foundation models and Transformer transfer learning.","figureLayout":"auto","heightBudget":null,"validation":"ok","generatedLatex":null,"slideNotes":"Conclude with the legacy of BERT and its adoption across commercial search and industry.","table":{"rows":[],"caption":"","hasHeader":true},"figures":[],"sourceIds":[],"grounding":null}]}],"assets":[],"ingestFiles":[]}') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO "WorkspaceSnapshot" ("id", "workspaceId", "savedAt", "label", "source", "revision", "snapshot") VALUES ('cmuac7i5700223i14ekyv167n', 'attention-is-all-you-need', '2026-09-20T21:37:27.452Z', 'Initialized project layout', 'human', 1, '{"id":"attention-is-all-you-need","name":"Attention Is All You Need","authors":"Ashish Vaswani, Noam Shazeer, Niki Parmar, Jakob Uszkoreit, Llion Jones, Aidan N. Gomez, Łukasz Kaiser, Illia Polosukhin","venue":"NeurIPS 2017 (Google Brain & Google Research)","userId":"user_3IGDYw03LkmHZaaCgKwWcBYxHQu","bibContent":"@inproceedings{vaswani2017attention,\\n  title={Attention Is All You Need},\\n  author={Vaswani, Ashish and Shazeer, Noam and Parmar, Niki and Uszkoreit, Jakob and Jones, Llion and Gomez, Aidan N and Kaiser, {\\\\L}ukasz and Polosukhin, Illia},\\n  booktitle={Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\\n  volume={30},\\n  pages={5998--6008},\\n  year={2017}\\n}","revision":1,"bibKeys":null,"agentEvents":[{"id":"init","ts":"07:45:18 PM","kind":"info","title":"Editor ready","detail":"Loading workspace…","status":"done"},{"id":"37268a38-9892-460d-a2e2-0d3402fc695b","ts":"07:45:52 PM","kind":"info","title":"Workspace loaded","detail":"7 cards · gemini","status":"done","createdAt":1789926352237},{"id":"5945d5a4-868e-4150-b563-bf5a97b44893","ts":"07:46:00 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789926360427},{"id":"946f2c69-87f9-41fd-b67f-8c064e8f18ad","ts":"07:46:05 PM","kind":"info","title":"Layout Inspection Passed","detail":"No visual overflows detected.","status":"done","createdAt":1789926365952},{"id":"6832a0a0-bf87-4f4f-b3b9-34018ecf3e1d","ts":"07:46:18 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","status":"done","createdAt":1789926378291},{"id":"f2d0b1d9-8de6-4f74-88dc-8d28a6d3a02d","ts":"07:46:29 PM","kind":"generate","title":"Compile succeeded","detail":"PDF ready for preview.","stat